Section 13 — datePicker: config.daysToolTipProperties setter
OLD_SECTION
var sourceKeys = Object.keys(value);
validateObject(value, ...);
if (sourceKeys.length > 2) { throw new Error(temp + " cannot be more than 2 properties"); }
for (let x = 0; x < sourceKeys; x++) {
    ... throw new Error ("... any of the keys: "+keys.join(", ") ...);

NEW_SECTION
validateObject(value, ...);
var sourceKeys = Object.keys(value);
if (sourceKeys.length > 2) { throw new Error("datePicker.config.daysToolTipProperties object value cannot have more than 2 properties"); }
for (let x = 0; x < sourceKeys.length; x++) {
    ... throw new Error ("... any of the keys: "+validKeys.join(", ") ...);

REASON
The loop compared x against the array itself (never ran, so key/type validation was
skipped); the over-limit branch threw on undefined `temp`; the invalid-key message
referenced undefined `keys` (should be validKeys). Also moved validateObject before
Object.keys(value) so a non-object input gets the intended error.

=======================================================================
Section 14 — slideSwitch: label rendering
OLD_SECTION
var labels = nativeCheckbox.dataset[hyphenatedToCamel(dataAttributes.label)].split(",");
labels[0] == undefined?"On":labels[0];
labels[1] == undefined?"Off":labels[1];

NEW_SECTION
var labelData = dataAttributes.label != "" ? nativeCheckbox.dataset[hyphenatedToCamel(dataAttributes.label)] : undefined;
var labels = labelData != undefined ? labelData.split(",") : [];
labels[0] == undefined ? labels[0] = "On" : null;
labels[1] == undefined ? labels[1] = "Off" : null;

REASON
With showLabel on (the default) and no label attribute configured or present, .split()
on undefined threw. The default expressions also computed "On"/"Off" and discarded the
result, so a missing second value rendered the literal text "undefined". Defaults are
now actually assigned, and a missing attribute falls back to "On"/"Off".

